      "text": "DNA Deamination Mediates Innate Immunity to Retroviral Infection\n\nHere, using a murine leukemia virus (MLV)-based system, we provide evidence that CEM15/APOBEC3G is a DNA deaminase that is incorporated into virions during viral production and subsequently triggers massive deamination of deoxycytidine to deoxyuridine within the retroviral minus (first)-strand cDNA, thus providing a probable trigger for viral destruction.\n\nThe cytidine deaminase CEM15 induces hypermutation in newly synthesized HIV-1 DNA\n\nHere, we demonstrate that CEM15 (also known as apolipoprotein B mRNA editing enzyme, catalytic polypeptide-like 3G; APOBEC3G), an endogenous inhibitor of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) replication, is a cytidine deaminase and is able to induce G to A hypermutation in newly synthesized viral DNA.\n\nStress-induced apoptosis associated with null mutation of ADAR1 RNA editing deaminase gene.\n\nHelicobacter pylori infection triggers aberrant expression of activation-induced cytidine deaminase in gastric epithelium",
